1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,320
I was rebuilding Circle's CCTP sample app

2
00:00:03,320 --> 00:00:07,180
for Arc, and I found a real bug.

3
00:00:07,180 --> 00:00:09,680
I reported it on the 4th of June.

4
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:13,610
Circle fixed it on the 24th. Twenty days

5
00:00:13,610 --> 00:00:15,480
for a thirty-line patch.

6
00:00:15,480 --> 00:00:17,280
Nobody did anything wrong.

7
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:19,720
That was just waiting for a human to have
time.

8
00:00:19,720 --> 00:00:22,370
So: if a bug can be stated as a checkable

9
00:00:22,370 --> 00:00:26,160
proposition, why is a human still in the
middle?

10
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:29,800
The agent never calls the target directly.

11
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:32,600
It goes through the Registry, and the

12
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:36,360
Registry does three things in one
transaction.

13
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:39,160
Ask the checker. Perform the move.

14
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:42,900
Ask again. Held before, broken now: pay
out.

15
00:00:42,900 --> 00:00:45,320
One transaction means no gap to steal the

16
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:49,440
reward. And it pays whoever found it, not
whoever noticed it.

17
00:00:49,440 --> 00:00:52,040
I type one command. From here on,

18
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:54,080
I do not touch the keyboard again.

19
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:03,500
It is reading the board off the chain.

20
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:07,880
This agent was told exactly one thing:

21
00:01:07,880 --> 00:01:10,310
the Registry address. Which contracts,

22
00:01:10,310 --> 00:01:13,580
which function, what they pay: all read at
runtime.

23
00:01:13,580 --> 00:01:15,950
And it discards what it cannot win.

24
00:01:15,950 --> 00:01:18,760
Already claimed. Rule already broken.

25
00:01:18,760 --> 00:01:20,990
It takes the one that pays most, and it

26
00:01:20,990 --> 00:01:23,960
names the runner-up, so you can see it

27
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:25,320
actually ranked them.

28
00:01:25,320 --> 00:01:28,800
Now it generates input. Not at random.

29
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:31,170
It works a boundary value list first, and

30
00:01:31,170 --> 00:01:34,200
every value carries the reason a tester
would try it.

31
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:38,280
Zero. One. One unit at six decimals.

32
00:01:38,280 --> 00:01:42,000
Values you try before knowing anything.

33
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:56,760
Probe six. One whole unit at 18 decimals.

34
00:01:56,760 --> 00:01:59,650
The rule broke. The Registry paid out

35
00:01:59,650 --> 00:02:01,600
in that same transaction.

36
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:05,600
The USDC is in the agent's own wallet.

37
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:20,800
That is the hash it just printed.

38
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:24,720
Paste it into the public explorer. Real

39
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:28,420
transaction, public chain.

40
00:02:28,420 --> 00:02:29,640
Anyone can check it.

41
00:02:29,640 --> 00:02:32,280
The question everyone asks: I wrote the

42
00:02:32,280 --> 00:02:33,920
target and the agent.

43
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:36,910
So how do you know it was not told

44
00:02:36,910 --> 00:02:38,020
where the bug is?

45
00:02:38,020 --> 00:02:40,540
This is the boundary list, straight from

46
00:02:40,540 --> 00:02:42,280
the file the agent reads.

47
00:02:42,280 --> 00:02:45,420
It holds no threshold from the target.

48
00:02:45,420 --> 00:02:48,220
One whole unit at 18 decimals is on it

49
00:02:48,220 --> 00:02:51,680
because that is the most common amount in
DeFi.

50
00:02:51,680 --> 00:02:55,180
Which is exactly why it sits at six, not
one.

51
00:02:55,180 --> 00:02:59,180
Same agent, pure random: zero hits in
10,000.

52
00:02:59,180 --> 00:03:01,800
The limits, before anyone asks.

53
00:03:01,800 --> 00:03:04,600
This does not replace an audit, and it

54
00:03:04,600 --> 00:03:06,800
does not protect live TVL.

55
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:09,180
On a contract holding real money,

56
00:03:09,180 --> 00:03:12,180
exploiting beats reporting, and nothing

57
00:03:12,180 --> 00:03:13,500
here changes that.

58
00:03:13,500 --> 00:03:15,560
What makes it work is not testnet.

59
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:18,360
It is zero assets at risk. On mainnet, the

60
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:22,000
same mechanism runs as a shadow
deployment,

61
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:24,000
paying real USDC.

62
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,520
The positioning: continuous QA for the

63
00:03:26,520 --> 00:03:27,740
release pipeline.
